1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,802
Eight AI agents just finished reading this
company's entire data room.

2
00:00:06,152 --> 00:00:11,454
Five findings. Filtering to the one that
matters: a single critical.

3
00:00:11,804 --> 00:00:19,516
Two high as well. Every row is a finding an
agent wrote, with its evidence attached.

4
00:00:19,866 --> 00:00:25,650
This critical one is the one that changes the
price. Opening it.

5
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:34,240
The target's largest customer can walk the
moment the deal closes, taking eighteen percent
of next year's revenue.

6
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:41,470
Ninety percent confidence, four source
documents, four contributing agents. No single
agent could call this alone.

7
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:48,700
I'm Divyansh Agrawal, and this is Diligence
Room, my entry for the Fortified Enterprise
Fleet track.

8
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:57,650
Every finding quotes the clause it came from,
and I can open the source document right here,
at that line.

9
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:05,806
Scope shows which agents contributed, and what
each one filed on its own.

10
00:01:06,156 --> 00:01:13,462
The finding graph traces it back: which
documents, which agents, which gateway decision.

11
00:01:13,812 --> 00:01:19,994
And the audit trail. Document parsed, finding
created, gateway decision, escalation.

12
00:01:20,344 --> 00:01:27,650
Any claim the fleet makes can be walked back to
the source text.

13
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:37,147
That was one finding. Here is the whole deal
room: coverage per workstream, documents
ingested, threats blocked.

14
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:45,127
And the escalation inbox, which is what the
fleet decided a human needs to see.

15
00:01:45,477 --> 00:01:55,650
Every document the agents read is listed, with
the workstream it was routed to and the
confidence behind that call.

16
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:07,014
Analysts are not the only thing reading these
files. I hit this fleet with twenty red-team
attacks.

17
00:02:07,364 --> 00:02:16,023
Prompt injection, encoded payloads, data theft,
cross-agent writes. All twenty blocked, zero
false positives.

18
00:02:16,373 --> 00:02:25,650
Gemma 4 screens every file, then Model Armor.
Nothing here ever reached an agent runtime.

19
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:35,814
The whole fleet is published into the Agent
Registry on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform,
discoverable company-wide.

20
00:02:36,164 --> 00:02:41,404
All eight, with versions, approval state and
eval scores.

21
00:02:41,754 --> 00:02:51,650
Memory Bank holds what we know about this buyer
across sessions, retrieved here in a fresh
process.

22
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:05,679
Now the part that decides whether you can run
this on a real deal. The negotiation agent can
draft, but it cannot send.

23
00:03:06,029 --> 00:03:14,052
I click draft. It writes the redline, quoting
the evidence it just showed me.

24
00:03:14,402 --> 00:03:22,424
It stops at pending approval. Nothing leaves the
building until a human says so.

25
00:03:22,774 --> 00:03:29,650
I approve it. Now, and only now, can the send be
recorded.

26
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:37,112
That approval lands in Cloud Logging as a
request against Cloud Run.

27
00:03:37,462 --> 00:03:45,728
Agent Runtime, Cloud Run in two regions,
Firestore and Pub/Sub, all live on Google Cloud.

28
00:03:46,078 --> 00:03:57,650
And the same call in Cloud Trace, span by span,
so an auditor can follow any claim back to the
source.
